       "similarity": 0.94,
       "inline": "AI companies, meanwhile, are spending many millions of dollars on the midterm elections, hoping to cow politicians in both parties. In 2025 alone, according to the New York Times , {+“+}AI companies, groups tied to the industry, and top executives [-in-]{+of+} both[- camps-] donated at least $83 million to federal campaigns.{+”+} Republicans have been benefiting more than Democrats because they’ve been more likely to resist regulation of the AI industry. {+As the Times reports, OpenAI co-founder +}Greg Brockman[-, a co-founder of OpenAI,-] and his wife, Anna, made four donations of $12.5 million apiece {+“+}to the leading super-PACs backing {+the AI industry and +}Donald Trump.{+”+} Democrats, however, are not being left out in the cold: Bay Area representatives Zoe Lofgren and Sam Liccardo took in at least $91,000 and $61,000, respectively, last year. Mark Warner, a Democratic senator from Virginia and a former tech executive, received at least $67,000, with another $82,000 flowing into joint fundraising committees associated with the senator, according to the Times . {+OpenAI CEO +}Sam Altman[-, the OpenAI CEO,-] cut a $7,000 check to Warner."
